00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:16.770
So, I come today really with, I would say

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.350
a slightly heavy heart.

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:20.800
In part, because when I
initially started teaching

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.020
this course on Blacks and Jews in America,

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:26.730
with two colleagues at
Haverford College back in 2009,

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:28.840
it was really more of
a kind of intellectual

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.840
sort of theoretical pursuit
to explore how history,

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.670
religion, ethics sort
of overlap and overlap

00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.280
with this particular set of individuals

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.780
or ethnic groups within the US.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.560
But something changed
after Trump's election

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.207
and with the Charlottesville

00:03:52.015 --> 00:03:53.432
protests in 2016.

00:03:56.529 --> 00:03:58.360
And as we sort of think about this moment

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.330
in terms of what changed
for me intellectually

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.460
and personally, I would
like to just have a moment

00:04:04.460 --> 00:04:07.070
of silence to remember
those who were murdered

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.710
at Mother Emanuel AME Church in 2015

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.340
and the Tree of Life Synagogue in 2018,

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:16.330
both groups were murdered
because they were black

00:04:16.330 --> 00:04:18.423
or because they were Jewish.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:26.070
Tywanza Sanders, Reverend
Sharonda Coleman Singleton,

00:04:26.230 --> 00:04:31.130
Reverend Clementa Pinckney,
Cynthia Hurd, Myra Thompson,

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.790
Reverend DePayne Middleton Doctor,

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:38.790
Susie Jackson, Ethel Lance,
Reverend Daniel Simmons,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:42.353
Joyce Fienberg, Richard Gottfried,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:47.670
Rose Mallinger, Jerry Rabinowitz,

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:51.740
Cecil Rosenthal, David Rosenthal,

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:56.740
Bernice Simon, Sylvan Simon, Daniel Stein,

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.663
Melvin Wax and Irving Younger.

00:05:09.819 --> 00:05:12.100
And how do we get here?

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:13.130
When I first heard

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.420
of the Unite to Right
Rally in Charlottesville,

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:19.610
I assumed it would be

00:05:19.610 --> 00:05:23.403
close to characters,
protesting immigration,

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:26.950
those, you know,

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.008
illegally entering the borders

00:05:29.008 --> 00:05:32.530
or something to do with gay marriage.

00:05:32.530 --> 00:05:33.650
This idea that, you know,

00:05:33.650 --> 00:05:35.980
liberals are taking over democracy,

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:37.870
but I was shocked, right?

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:42.830
When I discovered that at
the very heart of the rally

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:48.350
was about anti-blackness and antisemitism.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:52.560
And as my colleague, Jack
Butler Bali talked this over

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.460
after we watched this horrific clip,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.240
we were stunned by what we discovered.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.273
And I wanna take us back to that moment.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:09.543
So someone more capable like Donald Trump,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:11.600
who does not

00:06:13.570 --> 00:06:16.873
give his daughter to the Jew.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:20.623
So when we think about
blacks and Jews in America,

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:25.560
often this iconic picture
emerges in our mind,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.940
right, of Rabbi Abraham Heschel and King

00:06:28.940 --> 00:06:31.830
walking arm and arm in Selma in 1963.

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:35.660
And we think of the whole idea
of a grand alliance, right?

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:39.300
This idea of blacks and
Jews fighting arm and arm

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:41.590
against social unrest,

00:06:41.590 --> 00:06:44.920
fighting against anti-black
racism, antisemitism.

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:47.240
And we think of a very harmonious group.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:50.350
And then we think of a big
explosion happening in 67

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:51.770
with the emergence of Israel

00:06:53.202 --> 00:06:55.770
and this whole idea of black power

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:57.650
invoking black independence,

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:00.100
and then really in some
ways, frightening Jews

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.880
and pushing many of the
Jewish members of Snake

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.120
out of the organization

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.030
to build a kind of, sort
of self-reliant group.

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:08.780
And that is sort of the
kind of the narrative

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:09.750
we often hear about.

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:14.270
But I wanna argue that the
story is far more complicated.

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.830
And while we're not going
to all the details side,

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:18.350
I wanna argue that it's completed in part,

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:20.480
because someone like
Adolf Reed is gonna argue

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:23.380
that there are many different
types of relationship

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:24.330
between blacks and Jews.

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.950
We have elite blacks and
Jews working hand in hand,

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:30.700
right, at the turn of the
20th century with the founding

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.850
of NACP and also blacks and Jews working

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.257
with the Urban League, right?

00:07:36.257 --> 00:07:40.480
And these are African-Americans
and Jews who are, you know,

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.410
a part of, sort of the
establishment as it were.

00:07:43.410 --> 00:07:44.842
And if not a part of the establishment

00:07:44.842 --> 00:07:46.740
that definitely on the trajectory, right,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.330
of a middle-class sort of
ideology really thinking through

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:53.860
how do we work together, right,

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:58.400
to solidify our rights as citizens,

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.680
but also how do we work
together to end segregation

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.810
and antisemitism and
all the barriers right,

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:05.360
to achieving the so-called American Dream.

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.150
'Cause that's one aspect of this story.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:09.750
The other aspect of the
story really has to do with,

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:13.330
you know, day workers, right?

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:15.020
And sort of, you know,

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:19.830
Jewish merchants who are
often engaged in businesses,

00:08:19.830 --> 00:08:21.750
you know, because of
segregation at the time

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.130
permanently were forced to
work in black communities.

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:25.840
And so there's another relationship

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.800
between Jewish merchants

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.470
and say African-American
working class individuals.

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:33.380
And also African-American
women who are day workers

00:08:33.380 --> 00:08:35.910
working within Jewish homes.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:39.140
So we have these sort of two
tiered kinds of relationships.

00:08:39.140 --> 00:08:41.500
And so part of what I'm trying to do

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:45.870
in part as a result of
Charlottesville is sort of re-think

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.270
why is this material important to me?

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:49.920
And part of why I think it's important

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.360
is because I think
there's something in terms

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:54.820
within the kind of the infighting

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:57.800
within the sort of the
solidarity movements

00:08:57.800 --> 00:08:58.780
between the groups

00:08:58.780 --> 00:09:00.980
that we can actually
glean to help us think

00:09:00.980 --> 00:09:03.180
about public deliberation,

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:05.810
to help us really re-think
what is political solidarity

00:09:05.810 --> 00:09:08.480
and also help us to re-think
what are the grounds

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.670
for understanding religion
and sort of cross religious

00:09:11.670 --> 00:09:13.608
sort of dialogue.

00:09:13.608 --> 00:09:16.360
And so I wanna put forth an argument

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.760
that is really developed,

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:19.560
or at least started by Eric Sundquist

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.580
and argue that part of
the issue is this idea

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:24.250
that the Exodus right,

00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:29.250
is binding African-Americans
and Jews to the sort of really

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:31.770
ancient kind of history.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.750
And as many of you might recall an Exodus

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:35.467
it's like this notion
that "Thus says the Lord,

00:09:35.467 --> 00:09:37.637
"The God of Israel, let my people go,

00:09:37.637 --> 00:09:41.250
"That they may celebrate a feast to me."

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:45.690
And so this becomes a
kind of gathering call

00:09:45.690 --> 00:09:49.840
for both groups, sort of
to really think through

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:52.500
the promise of Yahweh, the promise of God.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.500
And I wanna argue that this
kind of relationship, right,

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:02.130
linking together blacks and
Jews through the Exodus story,

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:05.480
not the Holocaust or
black suffering in death

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:07.450
creates the possibility, right,

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:12.450
for thinking through texts as
lived possibilities, right,

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.830
texts as something that's live, not dead,

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.100
but also really to help
us think through the ways

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.170
in which disparate groups, right,

00:10:21.170 --> 00:10:26.170
can actually bracket immediate
social circumstances,

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.050
immediate economic circumstances,

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:33.060
and really try to link
to kind of a narrative

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:36.130
that is constant but shifting.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:37.150
In other words,

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:40.510
I wanna argue that blacks and Jews, right,

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:43.950
have within their fingertips,
this notion of Exodus.

00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:47.200
And if we read it in a
kind of a critical lens

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:50.460
through a kind of black
feminist, sort of a woman's

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:52.170
sort of perspective, right,

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:54.740
taking into account Eric
Sundqust's criticisms

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:59.470
of the Exodus narrative as
potentially an imperialistic,

00:10:59.470 --> 00:11:02.220
but also taking into account
John Levinson's account

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.540
that well maybe the Exodus story

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.850
really isn't about a
radicalization of the people.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:08.450
Maybe it really is about how people

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.840
become de-radicalized, right.

00:11:10.840 --> 00:11:13.810
I wanna argue that what
if we use this Exodus

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:16.020
as a way of thinking about
what does it mean to exist

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:16.853
in the wilderness?

00:11:16.853 --> 00:11:20.720
And what does it mean to exist as a people

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.560
who are dispossessed
and who are displaced?

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:25.810
And if we think about it
in these terms, right,

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.420
we're shifting the bait
away from this idea

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:30.750
of who's actually a chosen people, right?

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:33.460
We bracket that and we'll
be focused on this idea

00:11:33.460 --> 00:11:35.980
of what, how do we gain material freedom

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.490
for all human beings?

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:40.090
Because I think if we look
at particularly the works

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:43.440
of King and how King uses Exodus,

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.420
as well as Rabbi Abraham Heschel,

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:50.673
we see how the Exodus
becomes a kind of grounding

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:55.060
for live religion and in
live kind of expression

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:58.040
that really changes how we look at texts

00:11:58.040 --> 00:11:59.540
and how we look at doctrine

00:11:59.540 --> 00:12:01.800
and increase the possibilities
for looking at narrative

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:03.690
as something that is expansive

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.600
and a way of welcoming in
rather than using narratives

00:12:07.600 --> 00:12:12.253
to foreclose the very
possibility of a mutual dialogue.

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:17.900
You know, Abraham Heschel
was at a conference

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:21.860
and spoke of the ways in
which religion, right?

00:12:21.860 --> 00:12:24.410
And race work hand in hand.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:26.960
And he argued at a conference
religion and race in 1963

00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.110
that, you know, what Pharaoh was not dead.

00:12:30.110 --> 00:12:32.520
And part of the issue that we're facing,

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:34.770
particularly as American Jews,

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.570
but also white Protestants
that we have assumed

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:38.770
that Pharaoh is dead

00:12:38.770 --> 00:12:41.330
and that the work of
the Exodus is complete.

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:45.623
And if we look at what's
happening to the Negro in America,

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:49.760
you know, during the early and late 1960s,

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:53.660
we see this sort of resurfacing, right,

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:56.490
of a certain kind of evil
Abraham Heschel talks about,

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:57.970
which is racism.

00:12:57.970 --> 00:13:01.560
And that when we, as religious
people do not respond

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:05.170
to racism as a legitimate evil,

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.070
as something we much work against,

00:13:07.070 --> 00:13:09.587
we are turning our back to God, right?

00:13:09.587 --> 00:13:12.180
And this then takes us
back to this whole idea

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.940
of the Exodus.

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:15.750
And he wants to argue that, you know,

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:17.797
when we think about religion
and race, where he says,

00:13:17.797 --> 00:13:19.340
"Look, you can't worship God

00:13:20.767 --> 00:13:24.397
"And assume that the
Negro is the problem."

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:29.420
That the Negro is the
aberration of what it means

00:13:29.420 --> 00:13:30.610
to be human.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:35.610
And so he really wants to
use like religion and race

00:13:35.740 --> 00:13:39.260
in a very interesting way
that I think really beckons us

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.080
to really challenge the very grounds

00:13:42.568 --> 00:13:44.440
of American origin stories, right?

00:13:44.440 --> 00:13:47.527
Because many of us have been
taught that "Look, the nation

00:13:47.527 --> 00:13:50.400
"Is one of, you know, built
upon a city of a Hill."

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:51.610
Think of John went through.

00:13:51.610 --> 00:13:54.150
And that from that we get this idea

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.090
of American Jeremiad story and
of American exceptionalism.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:13:59.240
But what Heschel is pointing
to really are the ways

00:13:59.240 --> 00:14:02.000
in which we've used religion, right?

00:14:02.000 --> 00:14:03.370
Within a US context,

00:14:03.370 --> 00:14:06.610
in many respects to sort
of reify anti-blackness.

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:08.430
And I would even argue antisemitism,

00:14:08.430 --> 00:14:10.380
if we go back to James Baldwins article

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:12.690
in the "New York Times"

00:14:12.690 --> 00:14:16.550
that blacks are not
antisemitic, they're anti-white.

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:18.840
And so part of what Baldwin
is saying that I think

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.690
that Heschel is implicitly

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:24.300
aligning himself with is this
idea that we have not dealt

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:27.750
with the sort of lingering effects

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:31.700
of Christianity's ongoing,

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:35.950
but shifting uses of antisemitic tropes

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.520
and of anti-blackness.

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:41.780
And until we begin to deal
with what's at the very core

00:14:42.893 --> 00:14:44.380
of the American enterprise,

00:14:44.380 --> 00:14:47.270
both Heschel and Baldwin argue
that we're missing the point.

00:14:47.270 --> 00:14:51.830
And so, I want us to sort of
think about this whole notion

00:14:51.830 --> 00:14:54.320
of we did by anti-Semitism,
we have to also think

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:55.850
about anti-black racism.

00:14:55.850 --> 00:14:57.270
And we think about sort of like racism

00:14:57.270 --> 00:14:58.970
we have think about antisemitism and look

00:14:58.970 --> 00:15:01.450
at the sort of the core origins

00:15:04.196 --> 00:15:08.860
of both forms of hatred and
show how in many respects

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.720
what connects blacks and Jews

00:15:10.720 --> 00:15:13.070
is not the sort of mythic
history of, you know,

00:15:14.243 --> 00:15:15.190
we all have gotten along

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.460
and we all believe in the same things,

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.830
but it's this idea of we
both have a kind of common

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.270
sort of oppression around
the ways in which our bodies,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.290
right, for the policies and
this idea that Whoa, well,

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:28.310
Jews are dirty.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:30.560
So therefore they should
handle dirty money, right.

00:15:30.560 --> 00:15:32.200
For white supremacy in the modern context,

00:15:32.200 --> 00:15:35.060
this idea that, you know,
blacks are barbarians.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.520
So on the first level,

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.520
that then becomes a way
of connecting this idea

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:42.860
that both have experienced to
kind of anti-humanist, right.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:46.110
From, you know, Christian sort of tropes

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:47.770
and Christian writers.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:51.470
But then I think if we stay at
that level in terms of let's

00:15:51.470 --> 00:15:54.020
like, look at our shared
oppression, right,

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:55.230
then it gets really complicated

00:15:55.230 --> 00:15:58.690
because (Terrence sighing)
we begin then to compete

00:15:58.690 --> 00:16:01.690
in terms of, well, who's
actually suffering more?

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.370
You know, this idea, well,
whose Holocaust is greater?

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.630
Is it slavery? Or is it the Holocaust?

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.820
And I wanna argue that,
well, let's actually maintain

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.340
this notion of the kind
of shared oppression,

00:16:12.340 --> 00:16:15.580
but let's not use it as
the kind of foundation

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:16.950
of other relationship.

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.600
Let's actually then extend
it to look at how then might

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:25.290
the Exodus, right, as this
idea of dispossessed people,

00:16:25.290 --> 00:16:30.240
this idea of a people
who are landless, right.

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.630
People who are seeking a certain promise,

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:36.813
how does that then really
re-think what it means,

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:41.450
for us to think about political solidarity

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:43.653
within the US context.

00:16:46.522 --> 00:16:48.490
So I think this is important then

00:16:48.490 --> 00:16:51.070
also, because it really allows us then

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:55.240
the kind of theoretical
tools to, I think, engage

00:16:55.240 --> 00:16:56.220
in modern society,

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.363
but also to engage what
Michelle Moody Adams calls,

00:17:01.325 --> 00:17:02.780
What is Familiar, right?

00:17:02.780 --> 00:17:07.780
Often when we are sort of
debating about injustice, right?

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:11.050
Antisemitism, sexism, transphobia.

00:17:11.050 --> 00:17:14.380
We often start from what is very familiar.

00:17:14.380 --> 00:17:17.260
And I'm not saying that's
a bad starting point,

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:19.370
but I think we can't end there.

00:17:19.370 --> 00:17:22.900
And so part of what
invoking excess narrative

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:23.910
allows us to do,

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:26.410
I think is to bring in a
new set of sort of tools

00:17:26.410 --> 00:17:30.270
to think through the very
grounds upon which we

00:17:30.270 --> 00:17:32.090
are exerting, right?

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:37.090
Our sense of outrage about injustice.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:41.090
And so, part of what is
linking then King and Heschel

00:17:41.090 --> 00:17:43.960
is this idea of really
breaking the silence, right?

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:47.617
King argues that, "Silence
is a form of betrayal".

00:17:49.080 --> 00:17:52.920
And so, let's go back then
to what lessons can we learn

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:56.160
from how African Americans
have used this Exodus story

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:58.310
and how I'm trying to bring it together.

00:17:58.310 --> 00:18:01.730
One would argue that African
Americans have really viewed

00:18:01.730 --> 00:18:04.530
the Bible historically as
a kind of talking book.

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:08.900
There's an actual story in
archives of a slave who witnessed

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.500
a slave master reading this book.

00:18:11.500 --> 00:18:13.890
And he said, he literally
saw the book talking.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:15.860
Henry Louis Gates talks about this

00:18:15.860 --> 00:18:17.620
in his work "Signifying Monkey".

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:19.810
And from that moment on

00:18:19.810 --> 00:18:22.400
the slave went to the
Bible and wanted the Bible

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:23.670
to talk back.

00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:26.610
And so this became a kind of
trope with an African American

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:28.200
sort of religious folklore.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:33.200
And so when American
slaveholders told this story

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:35.550
of the Exodus to the slaves,

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:38.480
and connected to this idea
that you know of Paul's notion

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:41.060
that well, your inheritance
is really in heaven.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.937
So don't see the story
as liberating you now,

00:18:43.937 --> 00:18:46.130
but I actually liberates
you in the future.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:49.190
It's interesting the slaves,
we actually reject that story.

00:18:49.190 --> 00:18:52.770
We reject that interpretation
and we want to read the story

00:18:52.770 --> 00:18:57.110
as an example of what God can
do for one group of people

00:18:57.110 --> 00:18:58.930
is a reminder of what God can do for us.

00:18:58.930 --> 00:19:02.640
And so it became a way in
which they turn to the Bible

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.190
as a kind of talking book

00:19:04.190 --> 00:19:06.827
as this kind of form of engagement.

00:19:06.827 --> 00:19:09.577
And this thing created a
sense of radical hermeneutics.

00:19:10.832 --> 00:19:13.370
And that scripture became
a kind of living texts.

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:14.940
And this is important, I think,

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.110
because it will force
us to really re-think

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:19.040
both blacks and Jews

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:21.730
in terms of what are we holding as sacred

00:19:21.730 --> 00:19:23.840
that both binds us to our tradition

00:19:23.840 --> 00:19:25.280
and to what is sacred.

00:19:25.280 --> 00:19:26.550
And that those things are also necessary.

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:30.230
But then how then do we allow
for the very possibility

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:33.010
of rupturing that which we hold is sacred?

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:36.520
I think part of the answer

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:39.330
and the tools for this is
thinking about how slave

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.683
saw scripture as a kind of living text.

00:19:44.870 --> 00:19:48.460
And then next is idea, as
I mentioned before then,

00:19:48.460 --> 00:19:49.570
as a living text,

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:52.900
the slaves embrace Exodus
story as a kind of narrative

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:56.820
of God's promise for the future
and to use this as a guide,

00:19:56.820 --> 00:19:59.030
to think through the New Testament

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:02.980
and to think through their
own sort of material history

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:05.970
in terms of enslaved people.

00:20:05.970 --> 00:20:09.323
So then, from this notion, they
began to build a tradition.

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.250
And this tradition
includes the Sorrow Songs,

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:16.530
W.E.B. Du Bois talks
about this idea of really

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:18.543
the nation's first sort of, you know,

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:23.120
music that breathe life into the nation,

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:28.023
but also spoke of the life of slaves.

00:20:32.040 --> 00:20:34.980
And clearly then we have
the Fisk Jubilee Singers

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:36.520
and then Aretha Franklin.

00:20:36.520 --> 00:20:39.190
And so I wanna connect this
to a really interesting kind

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:41.380
of story in terms of how
we think about again,

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:44.760
texts as living and it's kind
of dialogical engagement.

00:20:44.760 --> 00:20:49.760
And there's a famous song by
that at Aretha Franklin,

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.790
it's called " Oh Mary, Don't You Weep".

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:54.460
And it's a song that sort of
links God's liberating hand

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:57.630
in the New Testament to Pharaoh's defeat.

00:20:57.630 --> 00:21:00.800
And what's also interesting
is that the songwriter

00:21:01.771 --> 00:21:04.240
and the earliest slaves sort
of weave together, right?

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:07.150
Both the Exodus story along
with the New Testament

00:21:07.150 --> 00:21:11.620
to really kind of create
this interesting exchange

00:21:11.620 --> 00:21:14.630
that links together both traditions,

00:21:14.630 --> 00:21:16.930
but then creates the possibility

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:19.010
for something new to emerge.

00:21:19.010 --> 00:21:20.900
And again, I think it
beckons to this whole idea

00:21:20.900 --> 00:21:22.200
that when blacks and Jews are working,

00:21:22.200 --> 00:21:24.470
whether it's at the elite level

00:21:24.470 --> 00:21:26.640
or even at this kind of
working class exchange,

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.910
there's a constant kind of navigation.

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:30.140
And part of that navigation,

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:31.500
at least on the part of African Americans

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.280
has everything to do with this idea

00:21:34.469 --> 00:21:37.210
of seeing the texts and
looking at texts in general,

00:21:37.210 --> 00:21:41.650
as a kind of talking book
and how this talking book

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:44.000
creates a kind of dialogical exchange.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.737
So I wanna read part of the lyrics to you

00:21:45.737 --> 00:21:48.410
and then play an excerpt
from Aretha Franklin.

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:49.243
It's not like a whole idea.

00:21:49.243 --> 00:21:51.140
Oh, Mary don't you weep don't you moan

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.590
Pharaoh's army got drowned in the red sea,

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:55.130
Oh Mary don't you weep.

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:57.380
If I could surely would,
I'd stand on the rock

00:21:57.380 --> 00:21:58.280
where Moses did

00:21:58.280 --> 00:21:59.620
Pharaoh's army got drowned,

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:01.810
Oh, Mary don't weep.

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:02.870
Oh, Mary don't you weep,

00:22:02.870 --> 00:22:05.320
tell Martha don't you moan, listen, Mary.

00:22:05.320 --> 00:22:09.080
And it's a story in terms
of what happens with Lazarus

00:22:10.203 --> 00:22:11.480
and the re-birth and there were Lazarus.

00:22:11.480 --> 00:22:14.680
I wanna just play a
quick excerpt of the song

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:17.250
to kind of invoke, right,

00:22:17.250 --> 00:22:20.790
this kind of exchange of the
singer and their audience,

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:23.053
how this speaks to this living tradition.

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:33.400
And so part of what I want to show,

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:38.400
we thinking through so to music, right.

00:22:38.630 --> 00:22:40.970
And that sort of exchanges
that will we hear in terms

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:45.480
of the sort of lead singer and the choir,

00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.020
and so the musicians is right,

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:51.140
this sort of constant exchanging
in terms of who has primary

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:52.840
leadership role, right?

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:55.880
This idea of trying to re-narrate a story

00:22:55.880 --> 00:22:59.070
around the promise, right.

00:22:59.070 --> 00:23:01.500
That people see within sacred texts.

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:05.670
And I think this in many
respects speaks to the ways

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:07.990
in which blacks and Jews,

00:23:07.990 --> 00:23:09.180
I think at the core, right,

00:23:09.180 --> 00:23:13.270
despite the kind of infighting
and the frustrations, right.

00:23:13.270 --> 00:23:17.270
They have the core, they are
sort of navigating, right,

00:23:17.270 --> 00:23:20.810
a set of texts to help them
think through how to make sense

00:23:20.810 --> 00:23:22.080
of the material world,

00:23:22.080 --> 00:23:25.040
but in a way that I think if
we keep those sort of Exodus

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:27.960
as a kind of living ongoing sort of story,

00:23:27.960 --> 00:23:29.260
that isn't dead,

00:23:29.260 --> 00:23:33.300
I think really speaks to the
ways in which freedom can be

00:23:33.300 --> 00:23:35.730
fully sort of embodied.

00:23:35.730 --> 00:23:38.370
And so, you know, Eric goes
and reminds us this idea

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.940
that, you know, that it's
extending from Rabbi Heschel,

00:23:40.940 --> 00:23:44.350
that Exodus is far from being completed.

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:47.850
And so, as we look at
say the role or the rise

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:50.890
of American Jews during the new deal

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:56.960
and with sort of the rise of
Jews as they left inner cities

00:23:58.092 --> 00:23:59.100
in the late forties and fifties,

00:23:59.100 --> 00:24:00.090
and moved to the suburbs

00:24:00.090 --> 00:24:03.820
and were embraced by,
you know, white Gentiles,

00:24:03.820 --> 00:24:06.430
there is on the one
hand, a story of, well,

00:24:06.430 --> 00:24:09.260
clearly Jews are kind of model minority.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:10.870
They are very successful.

00:24:10.870 --> 00:24:13.200
And so therefore they
don't experience, you know,

00:24:13.200 --> 00:24:15.300
the kinds of bigotry

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:17.160
that African-Americans have experienced.

00:24:17.160 --> 00:24:19.860
And I wanna join with
Eric Goldstein and argue

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.930
that what's interesting is
that even with this kind

00:24:22.930 --> 00:24:25.600
of sort of professional success, right,

00:24:25.600 --> 00:24:27.370
among many American Jews,

00:24:27.370 --> 00:24:30.200
what he wants to remind us
is that this idea that Jews

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:33.680
we have remained a kind of other.

00:24:33.680 --> 00:24:35.700
Well, they may not have been marginalized

00:24:35.700 --> 00:24:37.700
from the sort of the structures of power.

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:39.370
They remain a constant other,

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:42.210
which creates his own kind of
double consciousness, right.

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:45.520
And what we see with the
rise of Charlottesville

00:24:45.520 --> 00:24:46.680
is that that otherness

00:24:48.080 --> 00:24:52.530
is sort of materialized in
a kind of unspoken hatred

00:24:52.530 --> 00:24:55.320
and unspoken antisemitism that seems

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:57.660
to surface every few decades,

00:24:57.660 --> 00:25:00.770
especially when we're
experiencing some form of economic

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:02.173
or political trauma.

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:05.170
And then, I wanna link
that in terms of, well,

00:25:05.170 --> 00:25:06.270
what is otherness?

00:25:06.270 --> 00:25:08.640
How does that link to African-Americans?

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:09.830
What links us to idea,

00:25:09.830 --> 00:25:12.497
what Dubois call really the Negro problem?

00:25:12.497 --> 00:25:15.570
And so idea that in the
American context, right?

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:17.680
Blacks have been defined

00:25:19.229 --> 00:25:21.920
as a legitimate ontological problem.

00:25:21.920 --> 00:25:24.960
And the nation has been
working desperate to figure out

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:26.410
how do we solve this problem?

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:29.610
And that has been actually the source

00:25:30.486 --> 00:25:34.210
of what sort of reifies
anti-blackness is that people assume

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:35.920
blacks are the problem.

00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:38.110
And so what becomes interesting,
right, is that, you know,

00:25:38.110 --> 00:25:42.480
despite the kinds of
differences in terms of economic

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:45.260
and political success
between blacks and Jews,

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:46.433
there remains is kind of tension, right?

00:25:46.433 --> 00:25:48.750
There's kind of social tension around Jews

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:50.740
as a kind of perennial other

00:25:51.924 --> 00:25:56.924
and blacks as the kind of
perennially marginalized people.

00:26:00.907 --> 00:26:02.920
And so, as we think about the ways

00:26:02.920 --> 00:26:07.920
in which this marginalization
and otherness emerges,

00:26:08.740 --> 00:26:13.020
I wanna argue and again, this
is a work that is developing

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.680
that we have to re-think Israel

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:19.600
in the role of Africa, right,
in our respective communities.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:23.800
And I wanna join in with V.Y.
Mudimbe of Duke University

00:26:23.800 --> 00:26:28.800
and argue that we have invented
right, sacred places, right.

00:26:29.410 --> 00:26:33.380
As a way to affirm what is
familiar as a way to protect us

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:35.390
against white supremacy, right.

00:26:35.390 --> 00:26:38.240
As a way to legitimate
who we are as peoples

00:26:38.240 --> 00:26:41.280
to affirm our forms of civilization.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:43.500
But I think in doing so, right,

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:47.310
we have not explore through to
the dangers of what it means

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:51.330
then to create these sacred
places that then we then lack

00:26:51.330 --> 00:26:56.260
in a moment in history that
we lack as sort of a model

00:26:56.260 --> 00:26:57.180
for protecting us

00:26:57.180 --> 00:27:00.420
against a kind of
suffering a kind of death.

00:27:00.420 --> 00:27:03.780
And I think as long as we
keep these places right,

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:07.670
as a kind of trope or source
for protection, right,

00:27:07.670 --> 00:27:11.410
we will not be able to see the
possibilities of how they can

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:14.700
open up new possibilities for who we are.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.560
And I think we'll continue to have right,

00:27:17.560 --> 00:27:19.130
the Palestinian problem.

00:27:19.130 --> 00:27:20.570
So moonless large interface,

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:22.640
when also Africa we'll see right.

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:25.230
The continued problems
around poverty, right.

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:27.500
And around leadership.

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:29.840
And so what does it mean then?

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:32.810
How is Israel reinvented in
our lives and how's Africa

00:27:32.810 --> 00:27:35.430
reinvented into our lives?

00:27:35.430 --> 00:27:38.300
And so I made this, I'll
discuss this a bit later

00:27:38.300 --> 00:27:39.860
because we're running out of time here,

00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:42.290
but I want to bring in
sort of the rapper Drake

00:27:42.290 --> 00:27:44.173
to kind of think through for us,

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:49.040
what does it mean to kind
of think about reinvention

00:27:49.040 --> 00:27:51.920
of not only of who we are
as a sacred people, right.

00:27:51.920 --> 00:27:55.370
But reinvention, reinventing
the Exodus, right.

00:27:55.370 --> 00:27:59.900
As a way to call those beyond our sort

00:27:59.900 --> 00:28:04.470
of racial constructions and
to call us for what phenomenal

00:28:04.470 --> 00:28:06.810
call it kind of new humanism.

00:28:06.810 --> 00:28:10.793
And let me go here one second.

00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:20.663
So as we prepare to sort of think through,

00:28:21.890 --> 00:28:23.163
so the grounds of hope,

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:27.470
one of the reasons why I wanted
to sort of end with Drake

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:29.980
is in part to try to invoke the ways

00:28:29.980 --> 00:28:33.740
in which reinvention
right, happens all the time

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:36.640
and this idea of rethinking Exodus, right.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:39.680
I think it's actually necessary
because I don't imagine

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:42.420
a kind of quote grand
Alliance happening again.

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:45.530
I think the economic
disparities are too great.

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:47.080
I think for most of my students,

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:50.500
both white and white Jews
and African-Americans,

00:28:50.500 --> 00:28:53.030
they have no personal interactions.

00:28:53.030 --> 00:28:54.270
They don't live in the same neighborhoods

00:28:54.270 --> 00:28:55.940
they don't go to the same
sort of social functions.

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:58.630
They may work together, but
they don't have an intimacy.

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:01.580
But I think if we can focus on

00:29:01.580 --> 00:29:03.630
sort of the sacred texts, right.

00:29:03.630 --> 00:29:07.560
And use them as kind of these
sort of discursive sites

00:29:07.560 --> 00:29:12.560
for re-exploring the
ways in which we continue

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:14.820
to spread our wealth, or
despite our lack of wealth,

00:29:14.820 --> 00:29:18.363
continue to face a kind
of dispossession, right.

00:29:19.700 --> 00:29:22.070
Kind of landlessness,

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:25.150
as it were kind of wilderness experience,

00:29:25.150 --> 00:29:27.590
I think it opens the possibility then

00:29:27.590 --> 00:29:31.910
for using this history, not
necessarily regain an Alliance,

00:29:31.910 --> 00:29:33.230
if it happens, it would be great,

00:29:33.230 --> 00:29:34.350
but to find useful ways

00:29:34.350 --> 00:29:36.480
within our different communities, right.

00:29:36.480 --> 00:29:39.010
To fight against racism,
transphobia, right.

00:29:39.010 --> 00:29:43.102
The issues that are so did they
keep from becoming the full

00:29:43.102 --> 00:29:46.453
humans that Rabbi Heschel
talked about and again,

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:52.940
there are lots of, kind of,
I would say pitfalls here.

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:56.930
I mean, lots of, sort of
histories that are repulsive

00:29:56.930 --> 00:29:59.010
on both parts, and we can go through that

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:01.640
during the discussion, but I
want to focus on given the ways

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:02.630
in which, you know,

00:30:02.630 --> 00:30:04.883
we see this resurgence of white supremacy,

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:09.000
this resurgence of
anti-Semitism anti-blackness

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.050
I want to figure out how then
do we look at sort of the past

00:30:12.050 --> 00:30:15.116
as a way of remembering
the kind of Toni Morrison's

00:30:15.116 --> 00:30:16.220
way of cultivating, right.

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:18.380
We have dismantling, repairing

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:20.010
and re-imagining possibilities.

00:30:20.010 --> 00:30:21.400
I think the Exodus right,

00:30:21.400 --> 00:30:23.910
is the one story that sort of links us,

00:30:23.910 --> 00:30:26.580
but still recognizes our deep differences.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:29.240
So thank you very much for attending.

00:30:29.240 --> 00:30:31.643
And I look forward to
hearing your questions.

00:30:33.470 --> 00:30:37.170
- Thank you Terrence, for
that really thought provoking

00:30:37.170 --> 00:30:42.170
and very entertaining, I
have to say presentation.

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:44.943
And it just made me think that, you know,

00:30:45.851 --> 00:30:48.120
at the anti-Semitism program,

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:51.590
we don't actually have many
religious studies scholars

00:30:51.590 --> 00:30:53.800
talking and it's such like an important,

00:30:53.800 --> 00:30:55.650
different way of thinking, you know,

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:58.350
we mostly have historians
political scientists.

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:00.490
And so I thought it was
just very stimulating

00:31:00.490 --> 00:31:04.440
to have actually that way
of thinking presented to us

00:31:06.357 --> 00:31:10.040
to think about, you know,
such an important issue.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.670
So a lot of questions.

00:31:12.670 --> 00:31:15.450
So let's just kind of dive in.

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:19.930
One question I had is,
you know, I can't help,

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:24.930
but think that one of the
obstacles to a kind of black

00:31:25.150 --> 00:31:30.150
Jewish dialogue in America
comes from a contrast in the way

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:36.110
the two groups think about,

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:41.110
or kind of represent to
themselves, their story in America.

00:31:41.140 --> 00:31:45.850
So, you know, for black people, obviously,

00:31:45.850 --> 00:31:49.570
slavery is looming, you
know, extremely large,

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:53.920
and this kind of, America connotes,

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:56.430
oppression to a large extent,

00:31:56.430 --> 00:32:01.430
whereas for Jews, I think they tend to see

00:32:01.500 --> 00:32:04.440
their history in America,
as you know, itself,

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:08.230
an Exodus and a kind of
liberation, you know,

00:32:08.230 --> 00:32:09.710
to a problematic extent.

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:13.350
I just came from another
talk earlier today

00:32:13.350 --> 00:32:17.240
about the history of
anti-Semitism in America

00:32:17.240 --> 00:32:19.850
that I think Jews have a hard time

00:32:19.850 --> 00:32:23.280
even facing up to themselves.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:26.420
I think there's a kind
of tendency within Jewish

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:29.850
historiography, but also
popular Jewish culture

00:32:29.850 --> 00:32:33.070
to see America really,
as the promised land

00:32:33.070 --> 00:32:36.360
and to kind of whitewash
all the different ways

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:39.840
in which it was not always
the case that America

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:43.360
was so open to Jews, to the extent

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:46.430
what happened in Charlottesville
came as a total shock,

00:32:46.430 --> 00:32:49.880
you know, and of course in Pittsburgh too.

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:54.160
So I'm wondering if you can
address those two contrasting

00:32:54.160 --> 00:32:56.740
narratives of what America means.

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:58.640
- No, Maurice that's a great question.

00:32:58.640 --> 00:33:00.160
And there's so many ways to answer it.

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:02.420
I think, you know, what's
interesting that I think about

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:04.250
Michael stops 20 at the roots, right?

00:33:04.250 --> 00:33:07.930
This idea that for many
earliest Jewish families

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:10.410
was this whole idea when
we come to America, right,

00:33:10.410 --> 00:33:14.100
you stay below the radar, you
recognize the opportunities,

00:33:14.100 --> 00:33:16.930
but you also recognize sort
of the imminence of death.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:18.890
And what's also interesting
for African-Americans

00:33:18.890 --> 00:33:21.140
turns that issue around slavery, right?

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:25.170
It's really not until the
late 80s when Morrison

00:33:25.170 --> 00:33:27.900
introduces beloved that
African-Americans are be,

00:33:27.900 --> 00:33:29.950
are publicly talking about slavery.

00:33:29.950 --> 00:33:32.160
I mean, she argues that
she wrote the book in part

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:34.840
because black folk had buried the history

00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:36.130
and didn't want anything to do with it

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:40.380
because it was a public sort of mark

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:42.170
of their inferiority right.

00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:45.650
Of how they'd been captive in
a kind of a horrible history.

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:48.820
And so when beloved comes
out, I think Wendy's Eyelea

00:33:48.820 --> 00:33:49.960
really talks about this whole idea.

00:33:49.960 --> 00:33:50.793
Well, wait and are you trying

00:33:50.793 --> 00:33:51.900
to create a new Holocaust moment?

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:54.130
Because again, beloved comes out as we see

00:33:54.130 --> 00:33:56.850
the emergence of the
American Holocaust museum,

00:33:56.850 --> 00:33:59.310
and people are finally
talking about the Holocaust

00:33:59.310 --> 00:34:02.700
in America sort of role in
that, or a lack of sort of,

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:06.977
you know, stand up next to during the war

00:34:06.977 --> 00:34:08.550
and for African-Americans it was like,

00:34:08.550 --> 00:34:10.590
Oh, this is what American Jews are doing.

00:34:10.590 --> 00:34:12.640
Let us now then begin
to talk about slavery

00:34:12.640 --> 00:34:13.473
and very interesting.

00:34:13.473 --> 00:34:15.160
And it's kind of a similar way.

00:34:15.160 --> 00:34:16.520
And then there's another moment

00:34:16.520 --> 00:34:20.450
sort of prior to that in
1960s, where Eric Sundquist,

00:34:20.450 --> 00:34:23.730
is gonna argue that what
black power in some respects

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:25.250
allows Jews to say, wait a minute,

00:34:25.250 --> 00:34:28.780
I can also claim a kind
of self identity, right.

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:29.660
In my Jewish incident,

00:34:29.660 --> 00:34:33.470
my Zionist sort of background
as a way to exert myself.

00:34:33.470 --> 00:34:37.490
So some Rabbis, particularly
in the Midwest region

00:34:37.490 --> 00:34:39.240
are saying, and I'm sorry,

00:34:39.240 --> 00:34:41.570
Midwest in terms of mid
Atlantic region rather

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:44.300
are using the black power
as a way to try to ignite

00:34:44.300 --> 00:34:47.550
a kind of, sort of public
remembering of Jewish

00:34:47.550 --> 00:34:48.930
life in America.

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:51.250
And as you say to stop
whitewashing that history.

00:34:51.250 --> 00:34:53.370
So I do think so it's a kind
of a complicated history.

00:34:53.370 --> 00:34:54.930
So I think on the one hand,

00:34:54.930 --> 00:34:59.930
both groups publicly are masking
how they are internalizing

00:35:00.350 --> 00:35:02.430
and responding to white supremacy.

00:35:02.430 --> 00:35:05.712
And I think we do in a mascot
for different reasons, right?

00:35:05.712 --> 00:35:09.010
I think for African-Americans
is a site of shame.

00:35:09.010 --> 00:35:10.640
Like they, you know,

00:35:10.640 --> 00:35:13.180
they are trying to show
through the politics

00:35:13.180 --> 00:35:15.483
of respectability and
racial ideology and look,

00:35:15.483 --> 00:35:18.550
that we too can pull us
up by our bootstraps.

00:35:18.550 --> 00:35:21.550
And for American Jews is
a recognition that, well,

00:35:21.550 --> 00:35:24.800
I'm not white and I'm
discriminated against

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:26.370
at the same time, right.

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:29.140
I recognize what's happening
to African-Americans,

00:35:29.140 --> 00:35:30.780
but I also don't want, particularly

00:35:30.780 --> 00:35:32.250
for Southern Jews, right?

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:33.620
Many of them were merchant owners.

00:35:33.620 --> 00:35:34.453
I don't want to get killed.

00:35:34.453 --> 00:35:36.380
So there was a kind of
strange kind of silence,

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:39.240
but yet recognition
that I too am like them.

00:35:39.240 --> 00:35:41.590
So I think it's a really
kind of complicated history,

00:35:41.590 --> 00:35:46.010
but both are responding to a
kind of anti-ice, anti-Semitism

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:48.580
kind of type blackness and both,

00:35:48.580 --> 00:35:51.513
I kind of have this lingering
sense of death that's eminent.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:53.790
- Really interesting, okay.

00:35:53.790 --> 00:35:58.030
So here's a question from
a real expert on the topic,

00:35:58.030 --> 00:35:59.360
Cheryl Greenberg.

00:35:59.360 --> 00:36:00.690
- Oh my god, hello.

00:36:00.690 --> 00:36:03.384
Oh my gosh, Cheryl Greenberg
it's great to see you.

00:36:03.384 --> 00:36:04.860
Or have your question here.

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:08.080
- Yeah, so she says, this is
fascinating and persuasive,

00:36:08.080 --> 00:36:11.530
but how do we make sense of
the fact that most of the Jews

00:36:11.530 --> 00:36:14.120
who were most involved with civil rights

00:36:14.120 --> 00:36:15.880
were the least religious.

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:19.240
So reform leftist non-religious Jews,

00:36:19.240 --> 00:36:24.100
the Orthodox by contrast were
nowhere to be seen focusing on

00:36:24.100 --> 00:36:28.200
religious obligations or
approaches did not seem to tie

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:31.377
into Jewish engagement in the movement

00:36:31.377 --> 00:36:33.600
and the black Jewish women in the past.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:37.510
So how might it now, especially
with more Jews than others

00:36:37.510 --> 00:36:38.853
identifying with secular.

00:36:39.940 --> 00:36:42.140
- Wow, that's a great question of course,

00:36:42.140 --> 00:36:44.570
by the great scholar, thank you so much.

00:36:44.570 --> 00:36:45.907
I think you're right.

00:36:45.907 --> 00:36:49.290
And what I've been trying to
figure out is what role then,

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:50.500
how does her Abraham Heschel,

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:53.900
so play sort of writ large right within,

00:36:53.900 --> 00:36:54.980
so the Jewish community.

00:36:54.980 --> 00:36:58.770
And I do think we see in
terms of, at least in DC,

00:36:58.770 --> 00:37:01.700
some of the conservative Rabbis
are actually speaking out

00:37:01.700 --> 00:37:02.930
in favor, right.

00:37:02.930 --> 00:37:06.260
In sermons and maybe
they're not present in body,

00:37:06.260 --> 00:37:07.710
but some of their sermons indicating

00:37:07.710 --> 00:37:09.660
that they're recognizing what's happening

00:37:09.660 --> 00:37:11.450
to African-Americans in the 60s,

00:37:11.450 --> 00:37:14.250
and there's a kind of willingness
at least to articulate it,

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:16.680
that sort of concern within their sermons.

00:37:16.680 --> 00:37:18.660
Now I think you're right
in terms of the South

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:20.470
is a very different kind of story, right?

00:37:20.470 --> 00:37:22.750
There's a sense in which very real threat,

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:24.570
other fear of their death.

00:37:24.570 --> 00:37:25.980
And so what does it mean for this moment?

00:37:25.980 --> 00:37:27.370
Well, I know the New York Times

00:37:27.370 --> 00:37:29.280
had this great sort of
picture of acidic Jews

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.100
who held the signs of Black Lives Matter.

00:37:31.100 --> 00:37:32.570
And I think what you are
at a different moment

00:37:32.570 --> 00:37:37.060
now where conservative Jews in
particular recognizing right

00:37:37.060 --> 00:37:40.880
sort of the limits of sort
of the benefits of whiteness,

00:37:40.880 --> 00:37:42.970
right, because of this kind of othering.

00:37:42.970 --> 00:37:46.960
And I do think when we look
at what happened in Ferguson,

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:49.433
in Missouri, that a number
of conservative Jews

00:37:49.433 --> 00:37:50.770
were on the ground at, you know,

00:37:50.770 --> 00:37:53.450
our Rabbi at Georgetown gave me

00:37:53.450 --> 00:37:54.610
great anecdotal stories about this.

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:56.500
So I think, but the problem is,

00:37:56.500 --> 00:37:57.720
I think there's a different problem.

00:37:57.720 --> 00:38:01.340
I think we're seeing more of
the quote leadership right

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:04.680
within the Jewish community,

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:06.380
on the grounds of Black Lives Matter.

00:38:06.380 --> 00:38:09.280
And conversely, we're
seeing less leadership

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:11.270
in the black community
with Black Lives Matter

00:38:11.270 --> 00:38:12.770
more in terms of the youth wellness.

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:14.260
So, I think we've gotta figure out how do

00:38:14.260 --> 00:38:15.100
we make these bridges?

00:38:15.100 --> 00:38:16.990
And I'm actually later
today, later this evening,

00:38:16.990 --> 00:38:20.450
speaking to the Hebrew congregation in DC,

00:38:20.450 --> 00:38:22.350
my colleague and I and
Jack are gonna talk through

00:38:22.350 --> 00:38:24.740
some of these issues, but I
think now they should becomes,

00:38:24.740 --> 00:38:28.220
well, how do we like
transfer this information?

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:32.390
Or sort of the activism
from sort of the leadership,

00:38:32.390 --> 00:38:34.807
the Rabbis to the actual
sort of congregation.

00:38:34.807 --> 00:38:38.030
And that becomes a hard part
because my colleague Jack says,

00:38:38.030 --> 00:38:39.570
it's this whole idea of how do you reckon

00:38:39.570 --> 00:38:40.950
with this sort of history, right?

00:38:40.950 --> 00:38:45.200
Rabbi Stein Law, who was the Rabbi

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:48.650
at the conservative Israel here in DC

00:38:48.650 --> 00:38:52.090
where the late Ruth Ginsburg
attended had this great sermon

00:38:52.090 --> 00:38:54.110
a couple of years ago on, you know,

00:38:54.110 --> 00:38:56.260
Jews have to recognize they're not white

00:38:56.260 --> 00:38:57.310
and return to Judaism.

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:59.650
And again, for people who are not really,

00:38:59.650 --> 00:39:01.090
there's this whole sense
of, wait, are you saying,

00:39:01.090 --> 00:39:03.860
I need to become more religious
in order to have this sort

00:39:03.860 --> 00:39:08.200
of social justice sort of
Ethel's and an activism.

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:10.000
And he's like, yes,
because you're are Jews

00:39:10.000 --> 00:39:11.850
and you have to recognize
that's why you're here.

00:39:11.850 --> 00:39:13.560
So, and I have a problem with in part,

00:39:13.560 --> 00:39:15.410
because it speaks to the idea, well,

00:39:15.410 --> 00:39:17.930
there's a kind of burden then being chosen

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:20.070
that, and are you saying then that Jews

00:39:20.070 --> 00:39:22.410
have more responsibility right.

00:39:22.410 --> 00:39:24.830
To advocate for anti-bullying
or to fight against

00:39:24.830 --> 00:39:26.900
anti-blackness because of right.

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:28.960
This weight of being what
it means to be Jewish,

00:39:28.960 --> 00:39:31.250
according to this
particular kind of belief.

00:39:31.250 --> 00:39:33.780
So I hope that I know is scratches
to your question, Cheryl,

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:35.510
but I'm still thinking
through, but thank you.

00:39:35.510 --> 00:39:36.880
I think it was a great question.

00:39:36.880 --> 00:39:39.320
- Yeah, I mean, it's such
an interesting question.

00:39:39.320 --> 00:39:41.530
The question of Orthodox
Jews, because, you know,

00:39:41.530 --> 00:39:45.650
as you said, you know, there
used to be much more contact,

00:39:45.650 --> 00:39:47.970
I think between blacks and Jews.

00:39:47.970 --> 00:39:51.760
And now it's really Orthodox
Jews in certain neighborhoods

00:39:51.760 --> 00:39:54.470
in Brooklyn who have the
most day-to-day contact.

00:39:54.470 --> 00:39:56.720
And it's such an
interesting thought, like,

00:39:56.720 --> 00:40:00.370
would they be more receptive
to a religious, you know,

00:40:00.370 --> 00:40:04.320
approach to dialogue or less, you know,

00:40:04.320 --> 00:40:07.560
it would be really interesting
to know how they would,

00:40:07.560 --> 00:40:12.020
you know, hear what you're
saying about Exodus anyway.

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:16.607
So one other question I had is whether

00:40:18.860 --> 00:40:23.710
you can trace historically
some of the kind of decline of

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:28.240
dialogue to changes within
the black church in general,

00:40:29.750 --> 00:40:32.210
you know, over the last,
let's say 30 years.

00:40:32.210 --> 00:40:34.923
So, and maybe, you know,
do you see a kind of,

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:41.490
is the black church playing
a kind of less of a role now

00:40:41.490 --> 00:40:45.590
in shaping and, you
know, promoting dialogue?

00:40:45.590 --> 00:40:47.020
What do you think?

00:40:47.020 --> 00:40:48.060
- Well, that's a great question.

00:40:48.060 --> 00:40:51.320
I mean, when I, as I'm
getting calls, anecdotally,

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:53.770
I'm finding that it's real,

00:40:53.770 --> 00:40:55.900
I'm getting calls from
Jewish communities and Jewish

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:57.750
community leaders who want
to talk with this issue.

00:40:57.750 --> 00:41:00.780
And when I talked to colleagues and peers

00:41:00.780 --> 00:41:02.020
who are African-Americans are like, well,

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:02.853
why are we having this?

00:41:02.853 --> 00:41:04.730
Why do you want to talk
about blacks and Jews?

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:07.090
You know, they're white,
there's no connection, right?

00:41:07.090 --> 00:41:08.730
They're successful, we're not.

00:41:08.730 --> 00:41:13.730
And so it's this interesting
kind of shift in terms

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:14.780
of the conversation.

00:41:14.780 --> 00:41:18.440
I think in part of it has
to do, I think with, again,

00:41:18.440 --> 00:41:19.920
I'm not trying to blame it on a boogeyman,

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:22.439
but the ways in which
social structures, right?

00:41:22.439 --> 00:41:24.110
Forced, I mean, in many cases,

00:41:24.110 --> 00:41:26.040
forced Jews out of urban areas, right.

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:29.940
We have the case in Boston
where literally bankers

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:34.250
and legislator says, look, we
want to reclaim back Bay area,

00:41:34.250 --> 00:41:36.640
which is mostly African-American.

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.870
And we wanna actually moved into Roxbury.

00:41:38.870 --> 00:41:40.310
Well, that's where most
of the Jews were living.

00:41:40.310 --> 00:41:41.530
It was like, okay, well,

00:41:41.530 --> 00:41:43.250
we're going to basically
go and tell Jews, look,

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:44.900
the blacks are coming, they're
going to rape your women.

00:41:44.900 --> 00:41:46.981
And you're probably valuing and go down,

00:41:46.981 --> 00:41:47.814
you better get out of here.

00:41:47.814 --> 00:41:49.810
And so some people are like,
Ooh, I think you're right.

00:41:49.810 --> 00:41:51.040
And so it was this interesting thing

00:41:51.040 --> 00:41:54.180
where you have this happening in Roxbury.

00:41:54.180 --> 00:41:55.640
You also have happened in Philadelphia

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:57.320
that there's these shifts in urban areas

00:41:57.320 --> 00:42:00.740
that sort of forcing
the American white Jews

00:42:00.740 --> 00:42:02.150
out of these areas,

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:05.110
which is then basically then
leaving this black enclave.

00:42:05.110 --> 00:42:06.370
And some of these black enclaves

00:42:06.370 --> 00:42:08.310
are buying and purchasing on synagogues

00:42:08.310 --> 00:42:09.890
and converting them into churches.

00:42:09.890 --> 00:42:12.370
And so there's a lot of exchange here,

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:13.370
but something happens,

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:17.957
I think with the end
of black power, right,

00:42:18.820 --> 00:42:21.857
where we see this kind
of explosion over Israel.

00:42:21.857 --> 00:42:24.870
And then we have this period
between the 60s and the 80s.

00:42:24.870 --> 00:42:29.010
When we see again, a rise of
black mayors and major cities,

00:42:29.010 --> 00:42:32.510
you know, like Cleveland,
like Atlanta and DC

00:42:32.510 --> 00:42:35.900
irony is that many of the
mayors, their campaign managers

00:42:35.900 --> 00:42:38.437
were Jewish colleagues and friends, right.

00:42:39.370 --> 00:42:40.840
Jesse Jackson, right?

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:43.443
Famous anti-Semitic comments
behind me town, you know,

00:42:43.443 --> 00:42:47.090
his campaign manager was
an American-Jew, right.

00:42:47.090 --> 00:42:49.440
Who was really interested
in trying to sort of

00:42:49.440 --> 00:42:50.273
re galvanize the 60s.

00:42:50.273 --> 00:42:52.920
And so we have this
moment between 60s and 70s

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:55.520
where blacks are occupying public spaces,

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:57.370
but there's a huge decline, right.

00:42:57.370 --> 00:43:00.090
In terms of sort of black wealth.

00:43:00.090 --> 00:43:02.018
And there's a kind of stagnancy there.

00:43:02.018 --> 00:43:03.950
And there's also a sense in
which black mirrors are having

00:43:03.950 --> 00:43:07.750
to appeal right, to the
business community, right.

00:43:07.750 --> 00:43:09.710
Which is white Gentile and Jewish.

00:43:09.710 --> 00:43:11.780
So there's crazy, really kind
of strange relationships,

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:14.930
right, again, you're dealing
with American-Jews, right.

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:17.210
Who, you know, there's
some kind of commonality,

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:18.520
but they're not in your community anymore.

00:43:18.520 --> 00:43:20.410
Now they really are simply right.

00:43:20.410 --> 00:43:22.310
The face behind a building quote unquote,

00:43:22.310 --> 00:43:23.500
or your physician.

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:25.350
So it really changed the dynamics.

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:30.350
So I think for many of professional
American-Jews, you know,

00:43:30.500 --> 00:43:31.940
they remember the stories right

00:43:31.940 --> 00:43:34.200
around Abraham Heschel and King.

00:43:34.200 --> 00:43:35.727
And then for African-Americans
between like the 70s

00:43:35.727 --> 00:43:38.510
and 80s with the riser crack epidemic,

00:43:38.510 --> 00:43:39.700
they don't see that, right.

00:43:39.700 --> 00:43:42.260
They see great people
who have abandoned them.

00:43:42.260 --> 00:43:45.280
And so the idea of having a
conversation it's not really

00:43:45.280 --> 00:43:48.010
present now, will the leaders
have the conversation?

00:43:48.010 --> 00:43:50.220
Oh yeah, when I call them the ministers,

00:43:50.220 --> 00:43:52.300
oh, they're gladly have the conversation,

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:53.133
but the congregants are
like, well, I don't know,

00:43:53.133 --> 00:43:54.557
what do we get out of this?

00:43:54.557 --> 00:43:56.880
And so that's where my
culinary kind of stuck

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:58.500
in terms of how do we
get people on their own,

00:43:58.500 --> 00:44:00.230
particularly in black churches, right.

00:44:00.230 --> 00:44:02.000
Who want to have this conversation.

00:44:02.000 --> 00:44:03.440
Now in terms of Black Lives Matter

00:44:03.440 --> 00:44:06.550
what's interesting is a
number of the young advocates

00:44:06.550 --> 00:44:09.330
were saying, look, we'll
talk to white Jews.

00:44:09.330 --> 00:44:11.970
If they align with our political ideology

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:15.070
or we have some overlap,
let's have a conversation.

00:44:15.070 --> 00:44:17.460
So I'm finding that's
where people interested in,

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:20.930
for galvanizing and informing
kind of coalitions, right.

00:44:20.930 --> 00:44:24.140
Are in this sort of extensions
of Black Lives Matter, right.

00:44:24.140 --> 00:44:26.030
Which has a very
interesting history as well.

00:44:26.030 --> 00:44:27.880
So in some respects, you know,

00:44:27.880 --> 00:44:31.360
even though I'm arguing for
a retrieval of the Exodus

00:44:31.360 --> 00:44:32.730
the religious space, right.

00:44:32.730 --> 00:44:34.680
Is not where it's necessarily happening,

00:44:34.680 --> 00:44:37.360
but I still want to argue
that that language of Exodus

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:39.900
in terms of rethink dispossession, right.

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:42.560
A wilderness allows us
then to deal with sort of

00:44:42.560 --> 00:44:44.850
contemporary issues
right around Palestine,

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:46.960
right around this idea of refugee crisis.

00:44:46.960 --> 00:44:49.140
I think in galvanized both groups,

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:51.500
even though we're no longer in many cases

00:44:51.500 --> 00:44:53.280
in the same communities
or worshiping together

00:44:53.280 --> 00:44:56.720
or dining together in
the same social clubs.

00:44:56.720 --> 00:44:58.360
- Okay, we're running out of time.

00:44:58.360 --> 00:45:00.930
So I'm going to ask you
like two final questions

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:03.770
and you can kind of answer both either.

00:45:03.770 --> 00:45:05.960
So one, you know, from the audience,

00:45:05.960 --> 00:45:09.310
which picks up on something
that you've talked about

00:45:09.310 --> 00:45:13.300
you know, 1967 as this turning point,

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.420
both because of the Black Power Movement

00:45:15.420 --> 00:45:19.460
and also the six day war in Israel

00:45:19.460 --> 00:45:22.500
at the beginning of the occupation.

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:27.270
And, you know, whether you
see like intersectionality,

00:45:27.270 --> 00:45:31.140
for instance, now like an obstacle to,

00:45:31.140 --> 00:45:34.300
I think a lot of Jews
see that as an obstacle

00:45:34.300 --> 00:45:37.710
and how you know, think about that.

00:45:37.710 --> 00:45:40.940
And then another question
about the nation of Islam.

00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:43.850
So to what extent is your model predicated

00:45:43.850 --> 00:45:46.730
on a kind of Jewish Christian dialogue

00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:51.730
and how would you know, black
Muslims see that question

00:45:52.020 --> 00:45:55.210
and see the whole Exodus model?

00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:57.060
- No, both very good questions.

00:45:57.060 --> 00:45:58.350
And I'll start with the,

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:00.870
I guess the hardest one
first is relation of Islam.

00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:02.930
I mean, I would argue
that I see it as a kind of

00:46:02.930 --> 00:46:05.460
a post-Christian kind of model, right.

00:46:05.460 --> 00:46:08.220
In terms of really taking the Exodus story

00:46:08.220 --> 00:46:10.880
outside of the confines
of sort of what it means

00:46:10.880 --> 00:46:13.157
to have a sacred texts, sacred book,

00:46:13.157 --> 00:46:16.620
and really using it in terms
of public deliberation, right.

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:18.970
Using the kind of images, right.

00:46:18.970 --> 00:46:22.600
Not to reimagine a kind of exceptionalism

00:46:22.600 --> 00:46:23.900
we're kind of chosen us, right.

00:46:23.900 --> 00:46:27.350
But as a way to think
about in some respects,

00:46:27.350 --> 00:46:30.110
those who are not very visible, right.

00:46:30.110 --> 00:46:31.320
You know, in other words,

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:35.260
I'm trying to think of a model
of how then do we ignite even

00:46:35.260 --> 00:46:38.290
those who are wealthy or
don't have wealth, right.

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:42.020
To see that they have a
shared interest in terms of,

00:46:42.020 --> 00:46:43.817
if you believe in humanity, right.

00:46:43.817 --> 00:46:47.380
And where some form of humanity
can we then agree upon,

00:46:47.380 --> 00:46:48.960
right and we get to deal with issue

00:46:48.960 --> 00:46:50.170
on refugee crisis, right.

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:52.843
Because it speaks to the
very depths of who we are

00:46:52.843 --> 00:46:54.160
and what we think we imagine.

00:46:54.160 --> 00:46:56.620
So in some respects, I
want to take out the excess

00:46:56.620 --> 00:46:58.890
from the kind of religious sacred space

00:46:58.890 --> 00:47:01.370
and look at it in terms of a kind of

00:47:01.370 --> 00:47:03.290
working political vocabulary.

00:47:03.290 --> 00:47:05.300
And, you know, intersectionality
is really interesting.

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:07.620
My colleague and I we're
really beginning to rest

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:08.737
with this now because I
look for Jewish Americans,

00:47:08.737 --> 00:47:10.890
it's kind of in a basement, right.

00:47:10.890 --> 00:47:12.187
It does not work.

00:47:12.187 --> 00:47:13.400
And for African-Americans,

00:47:13.400 --> 00:47:16.230
it becomes a way of recognizing right.

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:18.160
The multiplicities and differences, right.

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:20.000
In terms of with gender sexuality, right.

00:47:20.000 --> 00:47:23.060
In class, that then allow us to see the,

00:47:23.060 --> 00:47:25.150
kind of the nuances of anti-blackness.

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:28.030
And so intersectionality works for us.

00:47:28.030 --> 00:47:30.110
And so now I think we need
to have comments in terms of

00:47:30.110 --> 00:47:32.990
why isn't it working for American-Jews.

00:47:32.990 --> 00:47:37.990
And I wonder is the question
around is what's is not working

00:47:38.580 --> 00:47:40.550
that question of whiteness, right.

00:47:40.550 --> 00:47:42.280
And in thinking through this idea

00:47:42.280 --> 00:47:46.420
of really exploring sort of
the conception of whiteness

00:47:46.420 --> 00:47:48.060
and the ways in which, you know,

00:47:48.060 --> 00:47:50.580
some American-Jews are clean to that.

00:47:50.580 --> 00:47:52.480
And really, I wanted to
bring into an bottom and say,

00:47:52.480 --> 00:47:55.280
look, you know, do you
want to choose whiteness

00:47:55.280 --> 00:47:57.160
or choose what it means
or just choose to be human

00:47:57.160 --> 00:47:59.270
because whiteness is a kind
of social construction,

00:47:59.270 --> 00:48:00.740
but it's a real culture construction.

00:48:00.740 --> 00:48:02.730
When we think about the early
citizenship laws, right.

00:48:02.730 --> 00:48:04.450
In order to, for the early immigrants

00:48:04.450 --> 00:48:06.140
to gain citizenship, right.

00:48:06.140 --> 00:48:09.262
In terms of a particular
salvation who came,

00:48:09.262 --> 00:48:11.780
they answer no, we actually,
we're not black or white

00:48:11.780 --> 00:48:12.750
we're part of the area, right.

00:48:12.750 --> 00:48:14.240
So you have to include us as citizens.

00:48:14.240 --> 00:48:17.830
So there was a real material good

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:19.540
to being white and historically.

00:48:19.540 --> 00:48:23.283
And so I understand part of
that kind of their concern.

00:48:24.710 --> 00:48:26.330
- Okay, well, you know,

00:48:26.330 --> 00:48:29.770
we could really go on
all day or all night.

00:48:29.770 --> 00:48:32.330
You have another talk you have to give,

00:48:32.330 --> 00:48:34.500
and I know people have to go eat dinner

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:37.690
so we will stop it there and just say,

00:48:37.690 --> 00:48:40.580
thank you so much,
Terrence, for that really,

00:48:40.580 --> 00:48:44.810
really stimulating and really
just very smart dialogue.

00:48:44.810 --> 00:48:46.280
So thank you very much.

00:48:46.280 --> 00:48:48.770
I will clap on behalf of everyone else.

00:48:48.770 --> 00:48:50.260
So thank you for coming.

00:48:50.260 --> 00:48:51.390
- No, thank you for the invitation.

00:48:51.390 --> 00:48:52.890
I had a great time, thank you.

